From 2e24e607279ef2f136d574ab0da5d022d024a90b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ophuscado <postbox@ophuscado.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 15:26:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] PCI: apple: Add the controllers tunneled over
 USB4/Thunderbolt

Each USB4 host router has a PCIe controller behind its PCIe down adapter
("apciec" in the Apple device tree). It has a single root port with the
T8103 register layout, at the same offsets on the M1 and on the M2
Ultra, but no reference clock or PERST# pin: the link is carried by the
host router and can only train while a PCIe tunnel is active.

Add a variant for it, "apple,t8103-usb4-pcie":

- The port is brought to the point where it waits for a tunnel at probe.
  Its application clock is exposed as a clock so that the port's IOMMU,
  which sits behind it, can only probe afterwards.
- A protocol adapter is registered for the root port node. When the
  Thunderbolt driver reports the tunnel, the tunnel reset is released,
  the link trained (retried up to five times), and the root port
  rescanned as a hotplug bridge. When the tunnel goes away everything
  below the root port is marked disconnected and removed, and the whole
  controller is reset through its power domain.
- The port's IOMMU is reset with the controller. It is kept awake
  across the reset and suspended afterwards, so that its runtime-PM
  callbacks neither touch it while it is unreachable nor skip setting
  it up again. The 2023 IOMMU driver had no runtime PM.
- The tunables the bootloader copies from the Apple device tree are
  applied when present: fabric and debug registers, port registers, and
  the root port's configuration space. The last target is inferred: the
  list starts like the internal controller's "pcie-rc-tunables", which
  m1n1 applies to configuration space. tunnel_tunables=0 turns them off.

The sequences are Sven Peter's, from the atcphy-20230219 branch of the
Asahi Linux tree, fitted to the current driver. Differences: one
function initialises the port at probe and after a reset, the tunables,
a link check that does not depend on the interrupt alone, the protocol
adapter is registered only once the host bridge exists, and only
exported PCI functions are used so the driver still builds as a module.

The internal controllers take the same paths as before.

Check: builds with clang 18 as a module (modpost clean) and built in,
no warnings. Not run yet.
